{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2312.00044, what factors make it difficult to set performance standards for AI audits, and how do these relate to the recruitment AI case study?", "answer": "Difficulty arises from defining and verifying fairness and accuracy, and these challenges are reflected in recruitment AI, where bias and accuracy indicators may conflict.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "For example, it may be assumed that the perspective of bias may not be emphasized in the indicators used to ascertain the accuracy of accurately determining which personnel are excellent and do not retire. Even if the model accurately identifies excellent human resources, there may be cases where discriminatory judgments are made."}, {"page": 14, "quote": "For example, various proof propositions (2.2) are envisioned for AI audits. Among them, some, e.g., fairness, are difficult to define precisely and difficult to verify. To illustrate, there are two major technical fairness indicators 39 , but they are not intended to be achieved simultaneously."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2312.00044, how does the scope of AI audits expand when considering both external libraries and training data governance?", "answer": "Including external libraries and training data governance broadens the audit scope to cover library providers, data collectors, and labelers.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "If the external library functions and their providers are included, the audit scope becomes even broader, without the assumption that the functions provided by these libraries are functioning properly."}, {"page": 19, "quote": "Because the validity of training data is essential in considering an AI audit, the audit scope becomes even broader when it includes such stakeholders and their governance."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2312.00044, what are the seven types of parties and organizations involved in AI auditing?", "answer": "The seven types are: AI service provider organizations, AI service users and user organizations, external auditors, standardization/certification/accreditation bodies, public institutions, private organizations, and other related parties.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "The following sections describe (1) AI service provider organizations, (2) AI service users and user organizations, (3) external auditors, (4) standardization bodies/certification and accreditation bodies, (5) public institutions, (6) private organizations, and (7) other related parties."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2312.00044, what does the 'three-line model' refer to in the context of AI service provider organizations?", "answer": "It refers to a governance model with three lines: development/operations, risk management, and internal audit departments.", "evidence": [{"page": 16, "quote": "The three lines for an organization providing AI services and systems are: 1) the department that develops and operates the AI system and service, and 2) the risk management department that checks and balances the development and operation of the AI services and systems. Management and the executive department, in their roles as the first and second lines, aim to enhance and improve their governance and management. The third line 3) is the internal audit department that independently verifies and audits the management and executive departments."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2312.00044, what is one reason companies in Japan may hesitate to undergo recruitment AI audits, not stated as 'lack of regulation'?", "answer": "High audit costs for wide-ranging verification areas may deter companies from using recruitment AI.", "evidence": [{"page": 24, "quote": "In addition, if the verification areas covered are wide-ranging, substantial audit fees will be paid to conduct a fullscale external audit. It is conceivable that some companies will stop using recruitment AI because the benefits are not worth the cost of external audits."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2312.00044, what is a challenge in evaluating the accuracy of recruitment AI decisions after hiring?", "answer": "It is not possible to retrospectively verify if rejected candidates were truly unsuitable, limiting accuracy evaluation.", "evidence": [{"page": 23, "quote": "Furthermore, while employers can after the fact review whether the AI's judgment was correct, they cannot after the fact review whether the rejected candidate was truly unsuitable, thus limiting the evaluation of accuracy to only some cases."}]}
]}
